course: dictionary
lesson: 7571
title: Words that change over time
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words change meaning over time, and that can confuse you. In old stories, «presently» often means soon, or after a little while. «I shall presently fall asleep» means you will fall asleep soon, not that you are falling asleep right now.
explain: Today, in news and formal writing, presently often means now, at the moment. «She is presently living in Toronto» means she lives there now. Use the words around it to decide: is the sentence talking about later, or about right now?
explain: Try the check. Swap in soon or now and see which one fits. «Perhaps he'll be better presently» works with soon: he will get better after a while. Phrases like «presently available» mean available now.
fact: «Presently» is an adverb.
fact: «Presently» means soon, or in a short time: «I'll be with you presently.»
fact: «Presently» can also mean now, at the moment: «She is presently living in Toronto.» This meaning is mainly US and formal.
fact: In older books, «presently» usually means soon, so it does not mean now there.
fact: Old word note: «presently» = soon after.
exercise: «I'll be with you presently.» What does presently mean here? | Never | Yesterday | *Soon, in a short time | Now, this very second
exercise: «She is presently living in Toronto.» What does presently mean here? | Far away | Soon, after a while | *Now, at the moment | Long ago
exercise: «It is a hundred to one that I shall presently fall asleep.» Which meaning fits best? | Never again | *Soon, after a little while | Quietly and slowly | Yesterday
test: Badger says, «perhaps he'll be better presently». What does presently mean? | Yesterday | *Soon, after a while | Only in the morning | Never
test: «The room is presently available.» What does presently mean here? | Long ago | Soon, after a while | Only in the past | *Now, at the moment
exam: Which sentence uses presently to mean now? | «We will leave presently.» | «Presently, the rain stopped.» | «I'll call you presently.» | *«The shop is presently closed for repairs.»
